#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/id #uuid "06e592bb-3ebe-57f1-a282-635599780593" :question/math-academy-id "e-8753" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/id #uuid "a9f1b212-7d45-566a-95d3-fa2697ad8c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9548" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "d659bb69-1a8d-5fb2-837c-27e454877021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9549" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,2)-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =(2(2)^{3}-{1}^{2})-(2(0)^{3}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =16-1+4 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a58e1191-8574-5c8b-aad0-40862378c6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168664} {:answer/id #uuid "5356e7c7-9d9a-545b-9282-aebe8737f899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168665} {:answer/id #uuid "553452ad-f992-5775-a833-df7a8b832245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168666} {:answer/id #uuid "57c36d72-0ad1-5646-a647-e8f45beb2daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168667} {:answer/id #uuid "691a50bc-f044-5162-bdd2-ffdf6c451472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168668}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf2eefd2-663e-5956-80a9-9cb987c41aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168663}] :question/id #uuid "77c64f72-bc4e-5149-99d1-c9f993fca3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125663" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = ⟨e^{x + y}, e^{x + y}⟩$ and the points $O(0, 0)$ and $A(1, \\ln 2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $F$ is conservative.\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 2e - 1$, where $C_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $OA^(―)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr = 2e - 1$, where $C_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y = \\ln (x + 1)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d45d42-4474-54e9-b538-3307c15f760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168676} {:answer/id #uuid "a440a693-552a-5180-ad1d-6490b565c2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168677} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd20a5f-397d-5846-8012-250cd73a8be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168678} {:answer/id #uuid "546811d2-f077-57fe-bdf8-11fc33d9d123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168679} {:answer/id #uuid "6aac6d3c-652c-5b99-bfe3-442ce8d4a895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "085f8263-e56a-56fc-a9dd-81a26c6fc695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168675}] :question/id #uuid "be0d9d11-b021-5cdb-982a-0da35bda23a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125727" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y) = 4x - 3y$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in $R^{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0625084b-e9ca-5f9d-9edb-7cd203c49e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256835} {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} {:answer/id #uuid "767cc64e-5a0a-54f8-b56e-81ea5d23171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257822} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf9ffb-d8c0-51f0-a661-b433dc2b60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257878} {:answer/id #uuid "e0403918-536a-524a-8c14-ab0744be6702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b90cfc-e328-5b69-bfa9-ca822accdfd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157b91e2-9d0a-5ac1-ac9e-d1e250dcce18" :question/math-academy-id "q-125748" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(1,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,-1)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{{1}^{2}}-(1){e}^{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8493c4a0-871c-5177-a611-79d399da4583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171676} {:answer/id #uuid "90ab2fa7-f0bb-5995-ad6a-bd36390a5a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171677} {:answer/id #uuid "d8825434-5542-58e1-8d22-d6f921f5fe04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171678} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf1d5fb-e457-5937-b9f7-3ecebe2aa922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171679} {:answer/id #uuid "658a6bb9-761f-5506-bb42-6b75e9c861d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "12d1fd06-9549-53ed-8960-7b958b76dab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171675}] :question/id #uuid "6f45f3fc-c4df-53d5-b37f-c7ba8162617a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98951" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y, z) = xyz + y^{2}$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1, 0,-1)$ to $(2, 1, 1)$ in $R^{3}$."}]}
